<Page x:Class="Umbra.App.Views.HelpPage"
      xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
      xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
      xmlns:tk="using:CommunityToolkit.WinUI.Controls">

  <ScrollViewer Padding="0,0,0,32">
    <StackPanel Padding="36,12,36,0" Spacing="0" MaxWidth="1000">

      <TextBlock Text="Help" Style="{StaticResource TitleTextBlockStyle}"/>
      <TextBlock Text="How Umbra works, and what it cannot do"
                 Style="{StaticResource BodyTextBlockStyle}"
                 Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"
                 Margin="0,4,0,28"/>

      <tk:SettingsExpander Header="Why hide a taskbar per monitor?"
                           Description="Windows 11 only offers one global auto-hide switch.">
        <tk:SettingsExpander.HeaderIcon>
          <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE7C4;"/>
        </tk:SettingsExpander.HeaderIcon>
        <tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
          <tk:SettingsCard ContentAlignment="Left">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="720"
                       Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}">
              The auto-hide checkbox in Settings applies to every taskbar at once. Umbra
              repositions the taskbar windows directly instead, so one monitor can stay
              clean while another keeps a normal bar.
              <LineBreak/><LineBreak/>
              On an OLED panel this matters beyond tidiness. A taskbar is static, bright
              and always in the same place, which is the exact recipe for burn-in.
            </TextBlock>
          </tk:SettingsCard>
        </tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
      </tk:SettingsExpander>

      <tk:SettingsExpander Header="Why is it fully off-screen, not a thin sliver?"
                           Description="Native auto-hide leaves a lit line. Umbra does not.">
        <tk:SettingsExpander.HeaderIcon>
          <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE7B3;"/>
        </tk:SettingsExpander.HeaderIcon>
        <tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
          <tk:SettingsCard ContentAlignment="Left">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="720"
                       Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}">
              Windows' own auto-hide keeps a one-pixel strip on screen, because the
              window itself has to catch the hover that brings it back. On the panel
              this was built against, that strip measured #434343 across three quarters
              of the width — a permanently lit line.
              <LineBreak/><LineBreak/>
              Umbra watches the cursor position instead, so the bar can go completely
              off the panel and light nothing at all.
            </TextBlock>
          </tk:SettingsCard>
        </tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
      </tk:SettingsExpander>

      <tk:SettingsExpander Header="Why can't I hide the primary monitor's taskbar?"
                           Description="Windows actively restores it. This is a hard limit.">
        <tk:SettingsExpander.HeaderIcon>
          <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE7BA;"/>
        </tk:SettingsExpander.HeaderIcon>
        <tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
          <tk:SettingsCard ContentAlignment="Left">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="720"
                       Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}">
              Moving the primary taskbar reports success, and then Explorer puts it
              straight back — measured within about 120 milliseconds. Only a tool that
              injects code into Explorer can override that, which Umbra deliberately
              does not do.
              <LineBreak/><LineBreak/>
              If you want a particular panel clean, make a different display primary in
              Settings › System › Display, then enable hiding here.
            </TextBlock>
          </tk:SettingsCard>
        </tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
      </tk:SettingsExpander>

      <tk:SettingsExpander Header="The taskbar is stuck off-screen"
                           Description="What to do if the engine dies unexpectedly.">
        <tk:SettingsExpander.HeaderIcon>
          <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE7BA;"/>
        </tk:SettingsExpander.HeaderIcon>
        <tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
          <tk:SettingsCard ContentAlignment="Left">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="720"
                       Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}">
              Stopping the engine from this app always restores the taskbars first, so
              this should not happen. If a bar is ever stranded — after a forced kill,
              say — restarting Explorer brings it back: open Task Manager, find Windows
              Explorer, and choose Restart.
              <LineBreak/><LineBreak/>
              The log on the Engine page records every start, stop, layout change and
              error, which is the place to look first.
            </TextBlock>
          </tk:SettingsCard>
        </tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
      </tk:SettingsExpander>

      <tk:SettingsExpander Header="Do my settings survive unplugging a monitor?"
                           Description="Yes — they follow the panel, not the port order.">
        <tk:SettingsExpander.HeaderIcon>
          <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE8A5;"/>
        </tk:SettingsExpander.HeaderIcon>
        <tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
          <tk:SettingsCard ContentAlignment="Left">
            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="720"
                       Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}">
              Settings are keyed on what the panel reports about itself — its
              manufacturer, model and serial number — rather than on names like
              "Display 1", which Windows reassigns whenever the set of attached
              monitors changes.
              <LineBreak/><LineBreak/>
              A monitor that reports no serial number falls back to its port, so its
              settings survive a reboot but not being moved to a different socket.
            </TextBlock>
          </tk:SettingsCard>
        </tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
      </tk:SettingsExpander>

    </StackPanel>
  </ScrollViewer>
</Page>
